un the migration, then read the catalogue for that organization.' apps/api/test/global-setup.ts:28 runs `npx prisma migrate deploy` against devscribed_test, and apps/api/jest.config.js declares that file as globalSetup; workers 2..N receive CREATE DATABASE ... TEMPLATE copies of the already-migrated database (global-setup.ts:60-64). Every migration is applied before the first test body executes. The Verification Plan row for this state points at e2e/global-setup.ts - the E2E harness, not the integration one - and marks it 'not run'. Two implementations both satisfy the words and differ in what they prove: (a) delete the organization's seeded RequestTopic rows, insert legacy-shaped Request rows, then execute a copy of the backfill SQL pasted into the test - which passes even when the shipped migration is wrong, because the copy is what ran; (b) migrate a second database to the migration before this one, seed it, then apply this one - which nothing in the harness supports. AC-2 ('an organization that existed before this spec has the same catalogue after the migration, with no request rewritten') is unobservable until the spec chooses.\",\n        \"source\": \"specs/requests/02-request-topics.cases.md#TC-02-INT-02; apps/api/test/global-setup.ts:28; apps/api/jest.config.js\"\n      },\n      \"suggestedFix\": \"Either restate TC-02-INT-02 in terms the harness can execute - name the fixture that produces a topic-less organization and require the case to invoke the migration's own SQL file by path rather than a copy of it - or move the case to a level that can migrate its own database and say so, and name the helper as a Verification Plan row that does not exist today.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P3\",\n      \"target\": \"spec\",\n      \"severity\": \"major\",\n      \"rule\": \"spec/ambiguous-requirement\",\n      \"file\": \"specs/requests/02-request-topics.contracts.md\",\n      \"symbol\": \"Screens / The list, amended\",\n      \"claim\": \"The amended list draws no Type filter while REQ-02-003's rationale rests on that filter existing, and the testid table neither retires it nor lists it. Whether requests-type-filter survives decides whether the vacation section keeps a selector.\",\n      \"witness\": {\n        \"kind\": \"scenario\",\n        \"detail\": \"REQ-02-003 Decided: 'the list's type filter already turns on this distinction, so a topic that could not declare it would leave the filter guessing.' The amended list wireframe in contracts #Screens draws '( Mine | All )  About [ Any ]  Status [ Pending ]  Project [ Any ]  search' with no Type control. The Required data-testid Attributes table adds requests-topic-filter, never names requests-type-filter, and is explicit about removal only for the modal: 'request-new-type and request-new-access-kind are removed'. In the code, apps/web/app/org/[orgId]/requests/page.tsx:334-340 renders requests-type-filter with options All types / Access / Question / Vacation, and the vacation value is not a filter over the requests array but the section selector of requests spec 01 requirement 41, asserted by apps/api/test/requests.spec.ts:1294-1303 ('type=vacation chooses the section rather than filtering one array'). Reading one: the control stays and the wireframe is incomplete. Reading two: About replaces it, and the vacation section loses its only on-screen selector, with no requirement, no case and no Blast Radius line recording the loss.\",\n        \"source\": \"specs/requests/02-request-topics.md#REQ-02-003; specs/requests/02-request-topics.contracts.md#Screens, #Required data-testid Attributes; apps/web/app/org/[orgId]/requests/page.tsx:334\"\n      },\n      \"suggestedFix\": \"State in the spec's own text whether the requests list still draws a request-kind filter after this change. If it does not, say what selects the vacation section instead and add the case; if it does, put requests-type-filter in the testid table beside requests-topic-filter.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P4\",\n      \"target\": \"spec\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"rule\": \"spec/ambiguous-requirement\",\n      \"file\": \"specs/requests/02-request-topics.contracts.md\",\n      \"symbol\": \"GET /api/organizations/{orgId}/request-topics\",\n      \"claim\": \"An unrecognised status query value on the catalogue read has no stated behaviour, while the sibling audience parameter has an explicit 400 and an explicit reason.\",\n      \"witness\": {\n        \"kind\": \"scenario\",\n        \"detail\": \"The contract gives status three values with active as the default. REQ-02-002 and TC-02-INT-04 make an unknown audience a 400 with REQUEST_TOPIC_MESSAGES.audienceUnknown, reasoned as 'The read refuses rather than returning everything, so a typo in a query string cannot look like an empty catalogue.' Nothing says what ?status=activee does: it either answers 400 or quietly returns the active catalogue, and both readings are consistent with the text. The repository's habit on the sibling endpoint is refusal - packages/validation/src/requests.ts:456 returns null for an unrecognised value and apps/api/src/requests/requests.service.ts:167 turns that into a 400 with the comment 'a closed set is only a contract if breaking it is observable' - which makes the silence read as an omission rather than a decision. Adjacent and equally silent: Validation Rule 6 says an out-of-range sortOrder is clamped, a deliberate departure from that habit, and says nothing about a non-integer.\",\n        \"source\": \"specs/requests/02-request-topics.contracts.md#Routes, #Validation Rules; specs/requests/02-request-topics.md#REQ-02-002; packages/validation/src/requests.ts:456\"\n      },\n      \"suggestedFix\": \"Say what an unrecognised status value answers, and what a non-integer sortOrder answers, in the Validation Rules table.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P5\",\n      \"target\": \"spec\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"rule\": \"spec/stale-premise\",\n      \"file\": \"specs/requests/02-request-topics.contracts.md\",\n      \"symbol\": \"Routes\",\n      \"claim\": \"The topic write routes name ManageRequestTopics in the Guards column and require a 403 body of REQUEST_TOPIC_MESSAGES.manageForbidden. The guard that reads that decorator emits a fixed, different message.\",\n      \"witness\": {\n        \"kind\": \"rule\",\n        \"detail\": \"apps/api/src/auth/capability.guard.ts:63-66 throws ForbiddenException({ error: 'forbidden', message: TEMPLATE_MESSAGES.generic.forbidden }), and packages/validation/src/documents.ts:125 defines that as 'You do not have permission to manage templates'. RequireCapability (apps/api/src/auth/require-capability.decorator.ts:14) takes a capability and nothing else, so no message can be threaded through it. Wired as the Guards column reads, TC-02-INT-07 - which asserts 403 REQUEST_TOPIC_MESSAGES.manageForbidden on all four write routes - fails on the body. The repository's answer already exists and is stated twice in comments: apps/api/src/holidays/holidays.controller.ts:19 ('Capability checks live in the service because ... CapabilityGuard can express neither combination') and apps/api/src/requests/requests.controller.ts:28 ('Capability is checked in the service rather than by CapabilityGuard wherever the spec names the message the refusal must carry, since the guard's message is fixed and generic').\",\n        \"source\": \"specs/requests/02-request-topics.contracts.md#Routes; apps/api/src/auth/capability.guard.ts:63\"\n      },\n      \"suggestedFix\": \"Say in the Routes table that the manage-request-topics check runs in the service and carries the named message, as the holidays and requests controllers already do, rather than naming a guard whose refusal is fixed.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"notes\": [\n    \"The plan is written and complete despite the block: 30/30 numbered requirements assigned to a task, 33/33 live test cases claimed, 22/22 '##' sections across all three bundle files accounted for by name, 27/27 required data-testid attributes carried. Eight tasks, T1..T8.\",\n    \"scripts/handoff-coverage.mjs passes on this handoff and its pass is not evidence. It reports 'requirements 0/0  cases 0/0  sections 8/8': it matches numbered requirements as /^(\\\\d+)\\\\. /m while this spec numbers them as '#### REQ-02-001' headings, and it matches '### TC-*' in run.spec alone while every case of this spec lives in 02-request-topics.cases.md. For a bundled spec in this format the gate sees neither requirements nor cases, and only the main file's headings.\",\n    \"packages/validation/src/requests.test.ts:332-360 drives validateNewRequest with a type and no topic and will break when the classifier is retired. The spec's Blast Radius enumerates the callers it breaks by design and names apps/api/test/requests.spec.ts, e2e/tests/requests.spec.ts and NewRequestModal.tsx but not this file. Not a defect - the enumeration is a mitigation, not a requirement - and it is in T1's file list.\"\n  ]\n}\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
